---
import {
  eslintSingleFileMs,
  eslintTypeAwareMs,
  formatMs,
  runFor,
  shownSeconds,
} from "../../../lib/benchmarks";
import Speed from "../speed.astro";

// The ESLint toolchain against both native engines, plus the single-file and
// type-aware timings from lib/benchmarks.
const small = runFor(42);
const large = runFor(420);
---

<Speed
  highlight="eslint"
  id="es-speed"
  stats={[
    {
      label: "To check the one file an agent just edited",
      unit: "s",
      value: String(shownSeconds(eslintSingleFileMs)),
    },
    {
      label: "To check 42 files",
      unit: "s",
      value: String(shownSeconds(small.ms.eslint)),
    },
    {
      label: "To check 420 files",
      unit: "s",
      value: String(shownSeconds(large.ms.eslint)),
    },
  ]}
  title="The trade-off is time"
  toolchains={["eslint", "oxlint", "biome"]}
>
  ESLint and its plugins run in Node, and Prettier and Stylelint each run as
  a step of their own. That’s seconds where the native engines take under
  half of one. In an agent’s loop it’s less, because the hook only fixes the file
  that changed. The type-aware preset adds TypeScript’s type checker, which
  took the 420 files to {formatMs(eslintTypeAwareMs)}.
</Speed>
